	"notes": "The New York Times homepage puts its front-page stories in a wide left column with a narrower rail of secondary stories and opinion pieces beside it — roughly a 70/30 split — and the whole thing is capped at 1200px and centred, so a big monitor just adds empty space on both sides rather than more news. Above all of that sits one band that breaks the pattern on purpose: an autoplaying video ad for a subscription offer that runs the full width of the browser, edge to edge, at every size tested — the only part of the page that is not capped. Below about 1024px the wide column and the narrow rail collapse into one single column, with the lead story first.",
